{"causes":["Ancient Babylonian astronomical knowledge was a key precursor to Ptolemy's work.","Ptolemy received training from prominent Egyptian astronomers.","The Roman Empire provided a vast market for Ptolemy's book, allowing it to spread widely across the Mediterranean."],"effects":["The Almagest contributed significantly to the development of modern astronomy by laying the groundwork for later discoveries such as the Copernican heliocentric model.","Its influence on navigation and cartography enabled sailors and explorers to chart more accurate courses and discover new lands.","The book's ideas shaped medieval Christian theology, particularly in the area of cosmology and the nature of God."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"claudius ptolemy","sourceName":"Claudius Ptolemy","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"Ptolemy wrote the Almagest around 150 CE."},{"sourceSlug":"byzantine empire","sourceName":"Byzantine Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"The Byzantine Empire helped spread the Almagest throughout Europe."},{"sourceSlug":"islamic golden age","sourceName":"Islamic Golden Age","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"Islamic scholars during the Islamic Golden Age commented on and expanded upon Ptolemy's ideas."},{"sourceSlug":"roman empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"The Roman Empire sponsored the production and distribution of the Almagest."},{"sourceSlug":"christian church","sourceName":"Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"The Christian Church played a significant role in preserving and transmitting Ptolemy's work throughout the Middle Ages."},{"sourceSlug":"egyptian astronomy","sourceName":"Egyptian Astronomy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"Ptolemy drew upon Egyptian astronomical knowledge when compiling his work."},{"sourceSlug":"babylonian astronomy","sourceName":"Babylonian Astronomy","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"Ancient Babylonians made significant contributions to the field of astronomy, laying some of the groundwork for Ptolemy's work."},{"sourceSlug":"greek philosophy","sourceName":"Greek Philosophy","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"almagest","targetName":"The Almagest","context":"Ptolemy drew upon Greek philosophical concepts when developing his geocentric model."}],"places":[{"name":"Alexandria, Egypt","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Almagest","type":"Reference article","slug":"almagest"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q155952","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almagest"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Almagest left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 63 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Almagest (Arabic: المجسطي, al-Majisṭī, from Greek: Μεγάλη Σύνταξις, 'Great Compilation') is the comprehensive astronomical treatise written by the Alexandrian mathematician and astronomer Claudius Ptolemy (c. 100–170 CE), completed c. 150 CE. It is the most important astronomical text of antiquity and one of the most influential scientific works in history, remaining the authoritative reference for mathematical astronomy in the Islamic world and Latin Europe for over 1,400 years — until the Copernican Revolution of the 16th century replaced the geocentric Ptolemaic system with the heliocentric model.

The Almagest's core achievement is a complete mathematical model of all celestial motions as seen from a stationary Earth at the centre of the universe — an Earth-centred (geocentric) cosmology with the Sun, Moon, and five planets (Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn) moving in a complex system of circular orbits, epicycles, equants, and deferents, combined to reproduce the observed motions of celestial bodies with remarkable precision. The model was sufficient for predicting planetary positions, eclipses, and the rising and setting of stars with accuracy adequate for navigation, timekeeping, and horoscopic astrology for over a millennium. The Almagest also contains a star catalogue (listing 1,022 stars with coordinates and magnitudes), a comprehensive treatment of mathematical methods (including the first systematic table of trigonometric chords, equivalent to a sine table), and discussion of geography and cosmology.

Transmitted through Arabic translations (the 9th century Arabic title al-Majisṭī — 'the Greatest' — gives the work its modern name) and then into Latin (the 12th century translation by Gerard of Cremona), the Almagest was the foundation of European astronomical education until Copernicus explicitly challenged it in De Revolutionibus (1543), Kepler replaced its circular orbits with ellipses (1609–1619), and Newton provided the gravitational mechanics (1687) that explained why the Copernican system worked. The Almagest thus represents both the pinnacle of ancient mathematical astronomy and the paradigm that the Scientific Revolution had to overthrow.","subjects":["Astronomy","Ancient Science","Classical Era","Mathematics","Greek Learning","History of Science","Geocentrism","Scientific Revolution"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Almagest had a profound impact on the development of Western astronomy and navigation, shaping the course of scientific inquiry for over 1,000 years.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
